WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:05.330
Everyone knows that Mary-Kate and Ashley Olsen
used their super twin powers to bring Michelle

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:07.260
Tanner to life on Full House.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:10.490
"No way Jose."

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:14.530
But a twin stepping in as a body double or
playing half of the role isn't just reserved

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:16.219
for fussy toddlers.

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:20.189
Identical twins have popped up in lots of
films and TV shows without you ever noticing.

00:00:20.189 --> 00:00:23.220
And while some on-screen doubles aren't quite
perfect fits…

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.960
"My name is Julius, and I'm ya twin brother."

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.080
"Oh, obviously!"

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.100
...you might be surprised at some of the roles
that were played by twins.

00:00:36.109 --> 00:00:38.559
Sophia Burset in Orange is the New Black

00:00:38.559 --> 00:00:43.489
One of the slickest twin twists in TV history
is in the Netflix smash, based on Piper Kerman's

00:00:43.489 --> 00:00:44.489
prison memoir.

00:00:44.489 --> 00:00:49.710
Creator Jenji Kohan and her crew tried to
use transgender icon Laverne Cox in her character's

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:51.710
pre-transition flashback scenes.

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:55.519
But the episode's famous director wasn't buying
Sophia as "Marcus" ...

00:00:55.519 --> 00:00:59.219
"And Jodie Foster directed this episode, so
I was like, 'I'm gonna have to butch it up,'

00:00:59.219 --> 00:01:02.620
because Jodie I don't think believed that
I could, I could pull this off.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:05.880
And I go to Jodie, and she looks at me and
she's like, 'we're going to have to hire someone.'"

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.310
Fortunately, they didn't have to look far.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:12.030
They decided to hire Cox's identical twin,
a composer and singer who performs under the

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.780
name "M. Lamar," who proved to be a perfect
fit.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:19.030
He had no previous acting experience or training,
but you wouldn't know it — his performance

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:23.750
and look was so seamless, most viewers probably
just assumed it was Cox in the role of Marcus,

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:25.130
aided by Hollywood magic …

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:29.799
"My friends were like, 'How did they do that,
was that like CGI or something?'"

00:01:29.799 --> 00:01:32.409
Sarah Connor in Terminator 2: Judgment Day

00:01:32.409 --> 00:01:37.340
Science fiction fans and critics raved over
Linda Hamilton's badass performance in T2

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:39.280
"You broke my arm!"

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.120
"There are 215 bones in the human body.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:43.120
That's one."

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:47.740
… and in a few scenes, audiences were graced
with double the Hamilton, most notably when

00:01:47.759 --> 00:01:50.270
the T-1000 mimics Sarah Connor.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:54.859
Director James Cameron didn't need to break
out the early-'90s CGI magic or pull an old-school

00:01:54.859 --> 00:01:57.410
Patty Duke split-screen...

00:01:57.410 --> 00:02:01.680
"One pair of matching bookends / Different
as night and day"

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:06.500
Cameron simply used Hamilton's identical twin
sister Leslie to get the shot.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.640
Xander Harris in Buffy the Vampire Slayer

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:12.840
In the season five episode "The Replacement,"
Xander lovers were treated to a double dose

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.520
of sarcasm and snark.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:16.060
"What if we do if this doesn't work?"

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:17.160
"Kill us both, Spock!"

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:24.640
To pull off the trick on a skimpy TV budget,
the show used a very lo-fi solution: Nicholas

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:27.410
Brendon's identical twin brother Kelly Donovan.

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:32.080
Brendon, however, still performed both roles
— Donovan only spoke when both Xanders had

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:33.330
lines in the same scene.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:34.880
"Cover your eyes!"

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:35.380
"This?"

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:36.470
"It will melt your brain!"

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:38.470
Claire Kincaid in Law &amp; Order

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:42.630
Actress Jill Hennessy was once so busy in
her role as assistant district attorney on

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:47.540
the long-running NBC procedural she literally
needed to be in two places at once.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:51.380
Fortunately, she has a twin who happily filled
Claire Kincaid's shoes.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:55.950
In 1996, Jacqueline Hennessy worked as an
uncredited stand-in for her sister in a few

00:02:55.950 --> 00:03:01.590
scenes in one season six episode because Jill
was busy filming a crossover episode of Homicide:

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:02.590
Life on the Street.

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:04.630
This wasn't Jacqueline's first time on camera.

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:09.640
The Hennessy sisters made their film debut
as twin escorts in the 1988 David Cronenberg

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:11.040
film Dead Ringers.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:12.780
Court Foster in The Man in the Moon

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:18.130
Actor Jason London, best known for his role
in 1993's Dazed and Confused, made his film

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:21.950
debut in the 1991 coming-of-age drama The
Man in the Moon.

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:26.150
His twin Jeremy — best known for Party of
Five and Mallrats — was actually the one

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:30.000
who wanted to audition for the film and convinced
his brother to drive him there.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.090
Jason read for the part, too, beating his
brother out … but Jeremy was still involved

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:35.170
with the production.

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:39.540
While Jason got to do all of the acting — including
kissing Reese Witherspoon in her film debut

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:43.790
— his twin brother Jeremy was on hand for
all the unglamorous stunt work … including

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:45.750
the infamous tractor incident.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:47.390
So...that sucks for him.

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:48.430
The Matrix

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:53.790
Though the 1999 science-fiction classic used
cutting edge special effects for some revolutionary

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:58.010
shots, the Wachowskis chose to go practical
for the agent training scene.

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:02.290
As Neo walks through the stream of people,
if you look closely, you can see that faces

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:03.400
keep repeating.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:05.780
That's because the crowd is full of identical
siblings.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:10.230
The directing duo said they couldn't afford
to pull off the effect digitally at the time,

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:14.370
so they ended up hiring as many twins or triplets
as they could find in Sydney, where the film

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:15.370
was shot.

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:18.949
So if you've ever wondered why that scene
felt extra creepy, there's your answer.

00:04:18.949 --> 00:04:20.069
Thanks for watching!

00:04:20.069 --> 00:04:22.830
